E.append(structure("artery-labyrinthine", "Labyrinthine (internal auditory) artery", "arteries",
 "The labyrinthine artery is a long, thin end artery, usually a branch of the anterior inferior cerebellar artery and occasionally of the basilar, which enters the internal acoustic meatus with the facial and vestibulocochlear nerves and divides into the cochlear and vestibular branches that supply the inner ear. The cochlea and the labyrinth have no collateral supply, so occlusion, embolism or vasospasm produces sudden unilateral deafness with vertigo, and because the artery arises from AICA, the same event often infarcts the lateral pons and the anterior inferior cerebellum. Sudden hearing loss in a patient with vascular risk factors, or hearing loss accompanying vertigo, should therefore be treated as a posterior circulation event until imaging proves otherwise.",
 "Arises from the AICA (about 85%) or directly from the basilar artery in the cerebellopontine angle, runs laterally with the seventh and eighth nerves into the internal acoustic meatus, and divides within the meatus into the anterior vestibular artery, the common cochlear artery and its vestibulocochlear branch.",
 "Supplies the cochlea (through the spiral modiolar artery), the vestibular labyrinth (saccule, utricle and the semicircular canals) and the nerves within the meatus; the anterior vestibular artery supplies the utricle and the superior and horizontal canals, the posterior vestibular branch the saccule and posterior canal, a division that explains partial syndromes such as isolated utricular or canal ischemia. Its stria vascularis territory maintains the endocochlear potential required for hair cell transduction.",
 ["artery-aica", "artery-basilar"],
 img([view("axial", -40, -35, -30, "internal acoustic meatus and cerebellopontine angle"), view("coronal", -40, -35, -30, "seventh and eighth nerves with the labyrinthine artery entering the meatus")],
     "Too small to see on routine MRA; the AICA loop in the cerebellopontine angle and sometimes entering the meatus is visible on high-resolution T2 (CISS/FIESTA) and is a normal finding. The inner ear itself shows as bright fluid signal in the cochlea and canals.",
     [pathol("Labyrinthine infarction", "MRI", "Loss of the normal bright fluid signal or abnormal enhancement of the cochlea and vestibule on delayed post-contrast 3D-FLAIR, often with an AICA territory infarct in the lateral pons and cerebellum on DWI.", sequence="DWI"), pathol("AICA occlusion", "CTA", "Absent AICA or basilar atheroma at its origin in a patient with sudden deafness and vertigo."), pathol("Vascular loop compression", "MRI", "AICA loop deep within the meatus contacting the eighth nerve, of debated relevance to tinnitus and vertigo.", sequence="T2")]),
 [("Sudden unilateral sensorineural deafness", "ipsilateral", "Cochlear ischemia (end artery, no collaterals)"), ("Acute vertigo with nystagmus and vomiting", "ipsilateral", "Labyrinthine ischemia, with or without cochlear involvement"), ("Lateral pontine syndrome", "ipsilateral", "Associated AICA occlusion: facial palsy, facial sensory loss, ataxia, Horner")],
 ["Weber and Rinne tests; sudden sensorineural loss is an emergency", "Head impulse test and nystagmus; look for the other AICA signs (facial weakness, corneal reflex, limb ataxia)", "Vascular risk factor review and posterior circulation imaging in sudden deafness with vertigo"],
 ["Hearing loss with vertigo is the signature of the labyrinthine artery and therefore of AICA; the PICA syndrome spares hearing", "The artery is an end artery: the cochlea dies within minutes of occlusion, and steroids are the only treatment for the idiopathic form", "An AICA loop in the meatus is a common normal finding on MRI"],
 [S(17, 466), S(17, 463, 486), B(12, 106)], synonyms=["internal auditory artery"], meshIds=["artery-aica"], subsystem="vertebrobasilar", parent="artery-aica",
 efferents=[("Cochlea, vestibular labyrinth, nerves of the internal acoustic meatus", None)],
 territories=["territory-aica"], syndromes=["syn-aica-lateral-pontine", "syn-hearing-loss", "syn-vestibular-neuritis"], pitfalls=["Isolated labyrinthine infarction has a normal brain MRI; the diagnosis rests on the clinical context and inner ear imaging"], tags=["end artery", "inner ear"]))

E.append(structure("nerve-sural", "Sural nerve", "peripheral",
 "The sural nerve is a purely sensory nerve of the calf formed by the medial sural cutaneous branch of the tibial nerve joined by the peroneal communicating branch of the common peroneal nerve; it descends between the heads of the gastrocnemius, pierces the deep fascia in the mid-calf, runs beside the small saphenous vein behind the lateral malleolus and supplies the skin of the posterolateral leg, the lateral heel and the lateral border of the foot to the fifth toe. It matters clinically far beyond its small territory: its sensory action potential is the standard measure of a length-dependent polyneuropathy, its preservation in a foot drop points to a root rather than a nerve lesion, and it is the nerve most often biopsied for vasculitis and amyloid, leaving a patch of numbness on the lateral foot.",
 "Formed in the popliteal fossa or upper calf from tibial and common peroneal contributions (the level of union varies), descends superficially in the posterior calf lateral to the Achilles tendon, passes behind the lateral malleolus with the small saphenous vein, and ends as the lateral dorsal cutaneous nerve of the foot.",
 "Carries cutaneous sensation from the posterolateral calf, the lateral ankle and heel and the lateral border of the foot including the fifth toe, with S1 as its main root. Its accessibility and constant position make it the nerve of choice for sensory conduction studies and biopsy, and its composition of mixed large and small fibers allows histological assessment of both.",
 ["artery-pca"],
 img([view("axial", -70, -50, -350, "sural nerve beside the small saphenous vein in the posterolateral calf (virtual)")],
     "Not shown on brain or spine imaging; on high-resolution ultrasound it is a small fascicular structure adjacent to the small saphenous vein in the posterior calf, and on MR neurography it is seen in the subcutaneous fat lateral to the Achilles tendon.",
     [pathol("Sural nerve enlargement", "US", "Hypoechoic swelling of the nerve in demyelinating neuropathies (CIDP, Charcot–Marie–Tooth) or a focal neuroma after ankle surgery.", sequence="n/a"), pathol("Nerve biopsy findings", "EMG/NCS", "Reduced or absent sural sensory action potential in length-dependent axonal neuropathy; biopsy shows vasculitis, amyloid or demyelination.", sequence="n/a")]),
 [("Numbness of the lateral foot and heel", "ipsilateral", "Nerve section or biopsy"), ("Painful neuroma", "ipsilateral", "Injury during Achilles or ankle surgery or after harvesting for grafting"), ("Absent sural response with normal superficial peroneal response", "n/a", "Not a lesion of the nerve: a pattern that helps separate S1 radiculopathy (sural spared) from tibial or sciatic lesions")],
 ["Test pin-prick on the lateral border of the foot", "Sural sensory conduction in any suspected polyneuropathy", "Compare sural and superficial peroneal responses when localizing a foot drop"],
 ["A preserved sural sensory response with sensory loss on the lateral foot means the lesion is proximal to the dorsal root ganglion: an S1 radiculopathy", "The sural nerve is the most biopsied nerve because it is expendable, superficial and representative", "Absent sural responses in the elderly can be normal; interpret with the clinical picture"],
 [B(17, 157, 166), B(27, 283, 287)], synonyms=["short saphenous nerve (old)"], meshIds=[], subsystem="lower-limb", parent="nerve-tibial",
 afferents=[("Skin of the posterolateral leg, lateral heel and lateral foot", None)], efferents=[("Tibial nerve (medial sural cutaneous) and common peroneal nerve (communicating branch) → sciatic nerve → S1 (S2) roots", None)],
 syndromes=["syn-length-dependent-polyneuropathy", "syn-radiculopathy-lumbosacral"], pitfalls=["The union of the sural components varies; in some people the nerve is entirely tibial or entirely peroneal"], tags=["sensory", "biopsy"]))

E.append(structure("nerve-pudendal", "Pudendal nerve", "peripheral",
 "The pudendal nerve carries the S2–S4 fibers that supply the perineum: motor to the external anal and urethral sphincters and the muscles of the pelvic floor and erection, sensory to the perineal skin, the external genitalia and the anal canal below the pectinate line, and the afferent limb of the reflexes that control continence and sexual function. It leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen, hooks around the sacrospinous ligament beside the ischial spine and re-enters through the lesser sciatic foramen into the pudendal canal on the lateral wall of the ischiorectal fossa. It is stretched in childbirth and prolonged cycling, blocked at the ischial spine for obstetric anesthesia, and entrapped in Alcock's canal to produce perineal pain that is worse sitting; its S2–S4 origin makes it the peripheral counterpart of the conus and cauda equina in sphincter localization.",
 "Sacral plexus (S2–S4) → exits the pelvis below the piriformis through the greater sciatic foramen → crosses the sacrospinous ligament medial to the pudendal vessels at the ischial spine → re-enters through the lesser sciatic foramen → pudendal (Alcock's) canal in the obturator fascia of the ischiorectal fossa → inferior rectal nerve, perineal nerve and dorsal nerve of the penis or clitoris.",
 "Motor fibers from the Onuf nucleus of S2–S4 supply the external anal and urethral sphincters, the levator ani, the bulbospongiosus and ischiocavernosus, giving voluntary continence and the rigidity phase of erection and ejaculation; sensory fibers convey touch and pain from the perineum, scrotum or labia, penis or clitoris and the lower anal canal, which drives the bulbocavernosus and anal reflexes and the sensory component of sexual response. The parasympathetic pelvic splanchnic nerves from the same segments, though separate, run alongside for erection and bladder emptying.",
 ["artery-pca"],
 img([view("coronal", -60, -60, -220, "pudendal nerve at the ischial spine and in Alcock's canal (virtual)"), view("sagittal", 0, -50, -160, "S2–S4 segments of the conus, the origin of the pudendal nerve")],
     "Not shown on neuraxis imaging; on pelvic MR neurography it is seen crossing the sacrospinous ligament medial to the ischial spine and running in the pudendal canal with the internal pudendal vessels. Its cord origin lies in the conus at T12–L1.",
     [pathol("Pudendal nerve entrapment", "MRI", "Thickening and T2 hyperintensity of the nerve at the ischial spine or in Alcock's canal, sometimes with a compressing vessel or scar; diagnostic block confirms.", sequence="STIR"), pathol("Conus or cauda equina lesion (central mimic)", "MRI", "Lesion at T12–L2 producing saddle anesthesia and sphincter loss that mimics bilateral pudendal neuropathy.", sequence="T2"), pathol("Pudendal terminal motor latency", "EMG/NCS", "Prolonged latency to the anal sphincter after stimulation at the ischial spine in obstetric or chronic stretch injury.", sequence="n/a")]),
 [("Perineal numbness and burning pain worse sitting, relieved standing", "ipsilateral", "Entrapment between the sacrospinous and sacrotuberous ligaments or in Alcock's canal"), ("Fecal and urinary stress incontinence", "bilateral", "Stretch injury of the pudendal nerve in childbirth denervating the external sphincters"), ("Erectile dysfunction and genital numbness", "bilateral", "Compression of the dorsal nerve of the penis (cycling) or perineal branches"), ("Absent bulbocavernosus and anal reflexes", "bilateral", "Interruption of the reflex arc: pudendal nerve or S2–S4 segments")],
 ["Perineal and perianal sensation, anal tone and voluntary squeeze", "Bulbocavernosus and anal wink reflexes", "Ask about the effect of sitting and cycling on the pain"],
 ["Perineal pain that disappears on standing and on the toilet seat is pudendal neuralgia (Nantes criteria)", "Bilateral saddle anesthesia with sphincter loss is the conus or cauda equina, not the pudendal nerve, until imaging says otherwise", "The pudendal nerve block at the ischial spine is the obstetric anesthetic of the perineum"],
 [B(17, 157, 159), S(14, 405, 408), C("snell", 4, 168, 172)], synonyms=[], meshIds=[], subsystem="lower-limb", parent="plexus-lumbosacral",
 afferents=[("Perineal skin, external genitalia, lower anal canal", "inferior rectal, perineal and dorsal nerves")], efferents=[("External anal and urethral sphincters, levator ani, bulbospongiosus, ischiocavernosus", "inferior rectal and perineal nerves")],
 syndromes=["syn-conus-vs-cauda-equina"], pitfalls=["Perineal sensory loss can be pudendal, sacral root or conus in origin; the presence of leg signs or a sensory level decides"], tags=["sphincters", "S2-S4"]))

E.append(structure("splanchnic-nerves", "Splanchnic nerves", "autonomic",
 "The splanchnic nerves carry the sympathetic outflow from the thoracic and lumbar cord to the abdominal and pelvic viscera, bypassing the paravertebral chain to synapse in the prevertebral ganglia. The greater (T5–T9), lesser (T10–T11) and least (T12) splanchnic nerves pierce the diaphragm to reach the celiac, superior mesenteric and aorticorenal ganglia, and the lumbar splanchnic nerves (L1–L2) reach the inferior mesenteric and hypogastric plexuses. They also carry the visceral pain afferents from the gut, whose cell bodies lie in the thoracolumbar dorsal root ganglia, which is why visceral pain is referred to the dermatomes of those segments and why a celiac plexus block relieves pancreatic cancer pain. The pelvic splanchnic nerves, despite the name, are the parasympathetic outflow of S2–S4 and are described with the sacral parasympathetic system.",
 "Preganglionic sympathetic fibers leave the T5–L2 intermediolateral column through the white rami, pass through the sympathetic chain without synapsing, and form the greater, lesser and least thoracic splanchnic nerves on the vertebral bodies, which pierce the crura of the diaphragm, and the lumbar splanchnic nerves from the lumbar chain; they end in the prevertebral ganglia around the origins of the celiac, superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric arteries and in the hypogastric plexus.",
 "Postganglionic fibers from the prevertebral ganglia follow the arteries to the gut, liver, pancreas, spleen, kidneys and adrenal medulla, inhibiting peristalsis and secretion, constricting splanchnic vessels to redistribute blood in exercise and hemorrhage, and controlling the sphincters; the greater splanchnic nerve's preganglionic fibers synapse directly on adrenal medullary cells to release catecholamines. Afferent fibers running with them signal distension, ischemia and inflammation of the viscera and mediate referred pain, and the splanchnic bed is the largest reservoir controlled by sympathetic tone, so its loss in autonomic failure causes orthostatic hypotension and postprandial hypotension.",
 ["anterior-spinal-artery"],
 img([view("sagittal", 0, -50, -120, "T5–L2 segments of the cord: intermediolateral column and the origin of the splanchnic outflow"), view("axial", 0, -50, -120, "thoracic cord: intermediolateral cell column at the lateral horn")],
     "The nerves themselves are not seen on neuraxis imaging; their cord origin is the lateral horn of the thoracic and upper lumbar segments. On body CT the celiac ganglia lie beside the celiac trunk as small soft tissue nodules, the landmark for celiac plexus block.",
     [pathol("Celiac plexus invasion", "CT", "Soft tissue encasing the celiac trunk and superior mesenteric origin from pancreatic carcinoma, the anatomical basis of intractable epigastric and back pain.", sequence="CT contrast"), pathol("Thoracic cord lesion above T6", "MRI", "Cord injury or transverse myelitis at or above T6 disconnecting the splanchnic outflow, with autonomic dysreflexia and neurogenic shock.", sequence="T2")]),
 [("Orthostatic and postprandial hypotension", "bilateral", "Loss of splanchnic vasoconstriction in autonomic failure or high cord lesions"), ("Autonomic dysreflexia", "bilateral", "Unopposed splanchnic sympathetic discharge below a cord lesion above T6 in response to bladder or bowel distension"), ("Ileus and constipation", "bilateral", "Sympathetic overactivity or loss of coordinated control after cord injury or surgery"), ("Referred visceral pain", "bilateral", "Convergence of splanchnic afferents on T5–L2 dorsal horn neurons shared with somatic dermatomes")],
 ["Lying and standing blood pressure, including after meals", "Abdominal examination for distension in cord-injured patients with hypertension and headache", "Map referred pain to dermatomes: epigastrium T5–T9, umbilicus T10, suprapubic T11–L1"],
 ["Autonomic dysreflexia needs a lesion above T6 because the splanchnic bed, the main sympathetic reservoir, is then beyond the reach of the brainstem", "A celiac plexus block interrupts both the sympathetic efferents and the pain afferents of the upper abdomen", "Referred pain follows the splanchnic afferents to the thoracolumbar dorsal horn, not the vagus"],
 [S(14, 390, 391), S(14, 387, 408), B(4, 41, 44)], synonyms=["thoracic splanchnic nerves", "lumbar splanchnic nerves"], meshIds=["spinal-cord"], subsystem="sympathetic",
 afferents=[("Visceral afferents from the abdominal viscera (pain, distension)", "run with the sympathetic fibers to the T5–L2 dorsal root ganglia")], efferents=[("Celiac, superior mesenteric, aorticorenal and inferior mesenteric ganglia; adrenal medulla", "greater, lesser, least and lumbar splanchnic nerves")],
 syndromes=["syn-complete-transection-spinal-shock", "syn-horner"], pitfalls=["The pelvic splanchnic nerves are parasympathetic (S2–S4), not sympathetic, despite the shared name"], tags=["sympathetic", "viscera"]))

